menu "PlantHub Swarm"

config PLANTHUB_WIFI_COUNTRY
    string "WiFi regulatory domain / country code"
    default "01"
    help
        The regulatory domain determines which channels a node may sweep
        during pairing/resync, and which channel a main hub's WiFi
        association is allowed to sit on. "01" is the IDF's world-safe
        default and restricts operation to channels 1-11. An actual ISO
        country code for a region that permits channels 12-13 (most of
        the EU, including Switzerland) enables them -- without this, a
        node can never find a hub whose router happens to land on
        channel 12 or 13, a silent and permanent pairing failure.

        WARNING: setting a country code for a region these devices do
        not actually operate in is a regulatory violation. Only set this
        to a code that matches where the hardware will really be used.

config PLANTHUB_ZIGBEE_BRIDGE
    bool "Zigbee bridge nodes (wireless Zigbee over ESP-NOW)"
    default n
    help
        A Zigbee bridge node is a swarm NODE whose radio role is zigbee: it
        runs its own Zigbee coordinator and forwards devices and readings to
        the hub over ESP-NOW. Retired 2026-10 in favour of the two-chip hub
        (C6 + a wired ESP32-H2 coordinator, CONFIG_PLANTHUB_H2_LINK_ENABLED),
        which needs none of this.

        When n (the default): the hub keeps no bridge table, routes no bridge
        commands and ignores bridge frames over ESP-NOW (swarm_bridge_* are
        "no such bridge" stubs, /api/v1/zigbee lists only the local/H2
        coordinator); a node never starts a coordinator and boots a stored
        `zigbee` radio role as `ble`; setting a node to `zigbee` is refused.
        Plain BLE/wifi_only nodes and the H2 path are unaffected. Saves the
        bridge table, its I/O buffer and the API scratch (~25 KB of static
        RAM) on every hub. Needs CONFIG_PLANTHUB_ZB_ENABLED=y to be useful.

endmenu
